    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0216",
      "sequence": 228,
      "meaning": "‘Tell me, lord: are these two beautiful boys ornaments of a sage’s line or protectors of a royal house? Or has Brahman, whom the Vedas praise by saying “not this,” come here wearing a double form? My mind, naturally the very form of detachment, grows transfixed like a moon-partridge. Therefore I ask you sincerely; please conceal nothing. Looking at them, my mind is forcibly abandoning even the bliss of Brahman for intense love.’ Smiling, the sage said the king had spoken well and his words could not be false: the boys were dear to every living being. Rāma inwardly smiled to hear this. They were born to Daśaratha, jewel of Raghu’s line, and the king had sent them for the sage’s sake. Rāma and Lakṣmaṇa, excellent brothers and abodes of beauty, conduct and strength, had protected the sacrifice—the whole world was witness—and defeated the demons in battle.",
